WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:00.300
You

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:01.768
might call

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.670
this The Godfather meets Washington.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.672
Instead of five warring mob families.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.140
It's a group of Republican lawmakers

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.541
from across their caucuses

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:09.542
political spectrum

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.277
trying to avoid a catastrophic

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.278
debt default.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:13.480
CNN reports the talks

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.415
between the so-called five families

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:16.750
and the House Republican leadership

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.851
are in their early stages.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:18.618
The goal,

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.653
some sort of consensus on the debt limit.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:21.721
What the lawmakers

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:22.322
and the leadership

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:24.157
hope is that they can force Democrats

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:25.558
to back off their calls

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:26.693
to raise the debt ceiling

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.328
with no spending cuts.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.330
CNN's David McKenzie and Melanie Sloan

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.731
join our conversation.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:34.134
So Kevin McCarthy

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:35.201
not afraid of The Godfather.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.737
He gets calls to five disparate groups

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:38.872
within the Republican family,

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.006
the five families

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.108
And the idea is this is hard.

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:43.143
If you want to propose

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.544
spending cuts in conjunction

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:45.111
with the debt limit,

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:45.345
that means

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.880
you've got to put them on paper.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.547
So you're trying to get

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:48.715
the most conservative members

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.116
to agree with the more moderate members.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.819
And so the strategy here for Kevin

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.220
McCarthy is really twofold.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.622
He wants to first show

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:56.289
that Republicans

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:59.192
can get 218 votes for any spending plan

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:00.360
because one of the chief

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.462
criticisms of Republicans has been

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.330
they can't negotiate among themselves.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.231
They could barely elect

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:06.499
Kevin McCarthy as speaker.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.467
Well, let alone

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.968
be negotiating with the White House here.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.170
So they're trying to strengthen

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.172
their negotiating hand by showing,

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.273
hey, we do have a plan,

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.207
we can come to agreement

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:15.141
and we can do this.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.043
And the second part of the strategy

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:17.377
for Kevin

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:17.844
McCarthy is

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.679
he wants to have an inclusive process

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.681
with these so-called five families,

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:22.615
a godfather

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.283
reference there

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.085
that I will say not all Republicans

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.553
love that reference,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.521
but McCarthy wants to show

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:28.755
that it's

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.523
not just going to be leadership driven.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:30.924
And that's

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.125
because he knows

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:33.426
that this is going to be a make

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.161
or break moment for his speakership.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.362
There is the power to oust him

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.397
at any given moment.

00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:39.199
So he is trying to sort of

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:40.166
thread the needle here

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.734
among the various wings of his party.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:42.535
But it's going to be a lot

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:43.770
easier said than done.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.537
And then if

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.239
and when they come to that agreement,

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.307
they still have to negotiate

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:47.974
with the Democrats.

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.609
And so let's just let's just go.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:50.043
You see them

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:50.877
up, up on the screen

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.879
there, Garret Graves as a top McCarthy,

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.247
I call him the leadership family.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.381
He's representing

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.149
the leadership in there.

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:57.383
Dusty Johnson,

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.651
is the chairman of what they call

00:01:58.651 --> 00:01:59.586
the Main Street Caucus.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:00.386
Patrick McHenry,

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.354
the Financial Services

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:01.921
Committee chairman.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.990
David Joyce, Republican Governors Group,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.191
Scott Perry, the Freedom Caucus.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.025
They tend to be

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.861
the more conservative members

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:09.896
so far in this piece ever.

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:10.430
This is Dusty

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:11.798
Johnson is the level of trust

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:12.465
and engagement

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.333
within the five families

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.567
that I've not seen in the previous

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:15.135
four years.

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.836
We're working really well together.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:17.403
Ralph Norman

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.805
of South Carolina, among those

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:19.939
who was not going to vote

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.407
for Kevin McCarthy back in the beginning,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:22.075
who ultimately did.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.309
We're trying to get to the framework

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.677
we all want buy in.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:25.778
There's a sense of that's the group

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:26.946
putting something out another group

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:27.514
put something out.

00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:28.114
They don't want that.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:28.748
They don't want to have

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:29.649
you know, you propose this.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.750
She proposes that.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.651
The question is,

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.019
you can start a conversation.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.821
Can you get to the finish line? Right.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:36.256
I would expect optimism

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.525
from some of those key negotiators there.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:39.926
And ultimately, Speaker

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.061
McCarthy is speaking to a concern

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.096
that was voiced

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:44.264
by Republicans

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:46.466
that power needed to be decentralized

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:46.833
means that

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:48.301
everyone should have

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.403
a role in these key negotiations.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.372
But the problem for Republicans

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:53.540
is going to be landing

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.909
on what exactly these cuts will be.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:57.510
This is a party that has,

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.344
I think, like

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.345
like to branded

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.614
the brand themselves on austerity.

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:04.150
But then when you get to the nitty gritty

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.152
and actually naming the programs

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.587
we just have not seen,

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:09.422
I think, a little political willingness

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.056
to go that far.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:10.690
And Republicans

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.158
get a lot more fiscally conservative

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:13.426
when there's a Democratic president.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.595
Just a funny coincidence in town.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.162
The Democrats

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.330
believe they have the upper hand here.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.631
This is the Senate majority leader, Chuck

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:19.299
Schumer, saying,

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.433
I dare you put it in writing.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.037
We have a clear position.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.805
Do it clean.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.741
Do it without brinksmanship, do it

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:31.344
without this risk of hostage

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.213
taking where things could blow up.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.747
He will not even say

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:35.515
what he wants to cut.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.183
And I'll tell you why

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.252
he can't pass a plan with cuts.

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:40.153
His hard right

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.921
will demand the kind of deepest cuts

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.022
that his more mainstream

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:45.959
Republicans won't vote for.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.627
Senator Schumer is not wrong

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.128
about the complications in the House

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:49.963
Republican family.

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:51.030
Here's my question, though.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.099
Can he keep the Democrats?

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:53.366
Can it

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:54.434
can he keep the Democrats

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:55.735
who in Trump states like

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:56.269
Jon Tester

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.237
from Montana,

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:58.571
Sherrod Brown in Ohio,

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.240
who have to run in 2024?

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.809
Can he he keep them from saying

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:03.810
let's do this clean?

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.812
We don't want any kind of spending cuts?

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:06.946
Well, that's one thing

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:08.081
that we're starting to see already,

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.415
whether it's Senator Joe Manchin

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.083
who has said that

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:11.818
he's fine with negotiating

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:13.086
and trying to encourage

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.320
President Biden to negotiate,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.655
even though the vast majority

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:16.289
of Democrats,

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:17.724
which is probably what

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:18.725
Majority Leader Schumer

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:21.027
speaking to do, want to do this clean.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.562
And also economists

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.230
whether they work for the US chamber

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.798
or elsewhere, have also said

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:26.633
they think it should be

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.034
a clean increase versus

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.003
tethering it to any kind of cuts

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:31.537
in terms of like Republicans

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:32.805
and what they in the past

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:33.973
have said they want to cut.

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:35.241
There's a long history there.

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:37.176
I mean, Paul Ryan, when he was speaker,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:37.744
very much

00:04:37.744 --> 00:04:39.178
wanted to turn Medicare

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:40.647
into a voucher system.

00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:41.881
And Republicans in the past

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:42.682
have said over and over

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:44.050
they want to cut entitlements

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:44.617
which include

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.786
Social Security and Medicare.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:48.521
So I know that Speaker McCarthy says

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:49.922
that isn't on the table this time,

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:50.757
but that's also why

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:51.991
President Biden feels like he has

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:52.325
his upper

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:53.426
hand is because of this long

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:54.360
list of history

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.561
and trying to push Republicans

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:56.896
to the name. Exactly.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:58.431
Very specifically what they want.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.033
And yet it is not that far

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.467
down the road where both Medicare

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:02.168
and Social Security

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:03.603
do need solvency help.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:04.937
They just do this as Senator Mike

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.472
Rounds, a Republican who says,

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:07.740
if we're having this political fight,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:08.941
we're not going to get to the substance,

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:10.743
which

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:11.411
is a golden

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:14.080
opportunity and in a bipartisan way

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.948
to put

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:18.451
Social Security on a long term plan

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.687
that would make it better in the future

00:05:20.687 --> 00:05:21.721
than what it is today.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.090
And to assure its longevity.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.892
But you do that by managing it.

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:26.492
You do that

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.628
by actually talking about it and not,

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.296
you know, doing doing dog calls.

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:31.898
Every time somebody says

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.400
let's try to address managing it.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.001
He's blaming

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:36.402
the president for dog calls now.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.203
But, you know,

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:38.638
the only way to do this in the past,

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.473
anyway, has been a bipartisan commission.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:40.740
Right.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:41.908
But but you have to go into it

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.176
with an open mind, meaning,

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:43.443
you know,

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.610
Democrats would have to go into it, that

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:45.678
maybe some programs

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:46.646
would be trimmed a little bit,

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:47.980
but Republicans would have to go into it

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.782
thinking maybe we need new revenues

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:50.550
to pay for all of this.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.051
We don't have that situation right now.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:53.086
And with the president highlighting

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:54.687
this so much, is it

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:55.955
am I dumb to believe

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:56.856
that this is gonna have to wait

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:57.623
until after the next

00:05:57.623 --> 00:05:58.758
presidential election?

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:00.993
Oh, if then you know this.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.296
This is a conversation

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:04.697
that comes up from time to time

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:06.132
about how to make Medicare

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:07.500
and Social Security

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.235
long lasting and strong,

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:10.570
which I think everyone agrees

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.238
they want it to be.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:14.941
But that is not an easy fix.

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:15.942
It's not the kind of thing

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:17.110
that can be negotiated

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.544
between now and June

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.680
when the debt ceiling comes up.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.316
And there is sort of a wasteland

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.851
of bipartisan commissions

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:25.651
that have

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:26.719
tried to

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.288
to find an answer to this challenge.

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:29.789
But

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.191
as the senator says,

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:32.692
you know,

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.160
the middle of a partizan fight

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:34.627
about the debt

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:36.262
ceiling is probably not the place

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:37.363
where that's going to happen.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.099
And the president told Speaker McCarthy,

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.068
I'll talk to you about spending cuts,

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.202
but only after you raise

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:43.736
the debt ceiling.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:44.370
Is there any way

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.638
the five families can agree on that.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:47.240
Keep it as two separate approaches.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.074
I mean, we'll see.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:48.374
That's what

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:49.575
they're trying to figure out right now.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.177
I do think that the goal right now

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:52.111
is to put up a plan

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.312
that would raise the debt

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.347
ceiling in exchange

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:55.548
for some spending cuts.

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:57.183
But like I said, putting that to

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:58.785
paper is a lot harder.

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:00.052
And putting your face

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:00.987
in front of these cuts,

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:02.755
the potentially popular programs,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:03.356
it can be difficult

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:04.957
for some of these moderates to swallow.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:06.092
And whether they realize it

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:06.426
or not,

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:07.126
John Reiff,

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:08.961
restructuring, reforming

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:10.396
entitlement programs,

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.598
that ultimately is a translation

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:13.766
to getting rid

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.201
of Social Security and Medicare.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:16.068
Whether they're not,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.136
they're clear on that yet.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:18.104
That's where it leans.

